WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:04.330
NahamSec is a name that needs no introduction.

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:09.530
He's both a top hacker and a top content creator
in the bug bounty space.

00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:15.910
And this gives him a unique perspective because
based on your comments, based on the questions

00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:21.560
you as the audience ask, NahamSec sees what
you are getting wrong.

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:26.690
And if you want to know what this is, then
enjoy the episode.

00:00:26.690 --> 00:00:27.690
Hello Ben.

00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:29.990
Thank you so much for joining me for the podcast
today.

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:35.550
To more people, you are known as NahamSec
and please tell us about your background and

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:38.130
how did you get to the point that you are
currently at?

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:40.579
Start wherever you want to start.

00:00:40.579 --> 00:00:41.579
Yeah.

00:00:41.579 --> 00:00:42.579
Hi, I'm Ben.

00:00:42.579 --> 00:00:45.079
Most people online, as you mentioned, know
me as NahamSec.

00:00:45.079 --> 00:00:51.270
I've been hacking for, or professionally I've
been hacking for about, I want to say 10 years.

00:00:51.270 --> 00:01:00.220
I got into bug bounties in late 2013 or early
2014 and I just heard people are getting money

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:04.589
from hacking companies and I was like, oh,
I can probably do this.

00:01:04.589 --> 00:01:09.420
Back then there was only a couple of companies
hacking, it was like Yahoo, Facebook, Google,

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:14.830
I think Coinbase launched like years later,
a year or two later.

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:18.150
Yeah, I just wanted, I was a college kid,
I wanted to figure out what I want to do.

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:20.220
I didn't like my degree really.

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:25.590
I didn't want to become a developer, so I
always loved hacking and I did it as pranks

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:31.720
from my friends and when I was a kid I pranked
my friends doing hacking or hacking with Sub7

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:34.659
and things like that when that was around
back then.

00:01:34.659 --> 00:01:40.300
And yeah, I got into bug bounties and then
later I met some folks at Bug Crowd, I met

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:41.930
some people at HackerOne.

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:45.899
I ended up getting employed at HackerOne and
I think getting employed at HackerOne kind

00:01:45.899 --> 00:01:50.990
of pushed me to do better and I really wanted
to build a community for hackers and having

00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:57.829
the access that I did with HackerOne, it made
it easier for me to make the community happen

00:01:57.829 --> 00:02:00.780
and meet everybody and collaborate with everybody.

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:06.130
So yeah, I want to say I'm a product of bug
bounties and I've had a career because of

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:08.920
bug bounties.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:13.880
Are you now doing bug bounty full time?

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:14.910
Full time-ish.

00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:20.920
I don't know if I would say I'm doing it full
time, full time, but it's one of my primary

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:23.290
sources of income.

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:27.129
I think I've gone to a point where I could
do part time bug bounty and still make a good

00:02:27.129 --> 00:02:30.840
living out of it, but I have multiple jobs.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:36.920
I did my pen test, I did bug bounties, I create
content, I offer trainings, I do public speaking,

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:40.360
I have a couple of other side hustles, but
bug bounties is still the thing that I enjoy

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:43.150
doing the most and it's given me a lot of
opportunities.

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:48.290
I still participate very regularly, but I
wouldn't say I'm spending 40, 50 hours a week

00:02:48.290 --> 00:02:51.120
like I used to back in the day.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:52.870
Yeah, of course.

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:57.390
It was the question I knew you will answer
more or less this because I'm also a bit annoyed

00:02:57.390 --> 00:03:01.260
when people ask me if I'm a full time bug
bounty hunter, like thinking that all the

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:06.310
time spent creating content and everything,
and you do even more things than I do.

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:12.379
So obviously, you can't be a full time bug
bounty hunter, but you don't work, you are

00:03:12.379 --> 00:03:15.380
not employed anywhere else, is what I meant.

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:16.860
I work for myself now, yeah.

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:22.379
I started working for myself back in November,
so a lot of people that know me mostly is

00:03:22.379 --> 00:03:25.849
from HackerOne, but I left HackerOne a year
and a half ago.

00:03:25.849 --> 00:03:31.480
I joined another startup, another Dutch startup
for the attack surface management stuff, and

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:37.319
then in November, things didn't work out and
I've been loving doing the content thing for

00:03:37.319 --> 00:03:41.060
a while and I think it was just the right
time to just push myself to do this full time

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:46.250
and it's given me the opportunity to do more
stuff for myself, more stuff for the community,

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:51.269
go back to hacking and just have a little
bit more freedom of what I want to do versus

00:03:51.269 --> 00:03:54.629
having to juggle a full time job and then
everything else that I was doing already.

00:03:54.629 --> 00:04:00.560
But yeah, I think the thing that a lot of
hackers don't understand is I think the first

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:04.829
couple of years is when you do full time hacking,
where it's considered like you work the, I

00:04:04.829 --> 00:04:08.790
don't want to say I did nine to five, but
you're putting a good eight, nine, ten hours

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:14.800
a day because not only you're learning, but
you want to hack to learn, right?

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:18.229
You're learning and then you're doing hands-on
thing with bug bounties and as more and more

00:04:18.229 --> 00:04:21.380
you do those things, it becomes more natural.

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:25.570
Some steps are automated in the past couple
of years or some things you understand better

00:04:25.570 --> 00:04:28.800
and you grasp the idea quicker or you know
if they're vulnerable and things like that.

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:32.630
So it just becomes, I think the more experience
you gain, the less time you spend.

00:04:32.630 --> 00:04:36.530
But I mean, I know hackers that are spending
enormous amount of times and making hundreds

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:38.850
of thousands a month.

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:42.699
Yeah, we'll definitely come back to this in
a second.

00:04:42.699 --> 00:04:46.539
But tell me what did you do at HackerOne?

00:04:46.539 --> 00:04:51.699
So I joined HackerOne as an intern and before
I left, I was the head of hacker education

00:04:51.699 --> 00:04:54.160
for HackerOne.

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:59.440
I worked from anywhere in the beginning from
working off with our sales team to help bring

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:01.720
in customers, educate customers.

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:04.250
I was the first person to do triage at HackerOne.

00:05:04.250 --> 00:05:07.420
I think outside of the co-founders, I was
the first one that was hired on the triage

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:08.420
team.

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:11.919
I helped build the triage team a little bit
or be a part of it.

00:05:11.919 --> 00:05:15.180
And then I worked on the customer service
team.

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:19.110
And then eventually I realized like I like
to go to conferences and I like educating

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:24.550
people and I asked my boss, Luke Tucker was
my boss at the time that I wanted to do it.

00:05:24.550 --> 00:05:29.669
He actually offered me to do education and
it was kind of a hard thing to shift over

00:05:29.669 --> 00:05:34.690
to because it's no longer as technical as
it sounds because you're doing community work.

00:05:34.690 --> 00:05:38.660
But it was something that I really enjoyed
and it was more of a creating CTFs to teach

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:44.280
people or creating content to teach people
or outsourcing content to be created for Hacker101.

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:49.480
So everything you see on Hacker101, it was
done by me and somebody else, Dakin, who was

00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:52.979
in charge of Hacker101 before they left.

00:05:52.979 --> 00:05:57.650
And then I took over and like most of the
CTFs that were newer were designed by me and

00:05:57.650 --> 00:06:04.870
then created by Adam Langley, who was the
CTF person that coded the CTF versus the stuff

00:06:04.870 --> 00:06:08.280
that I designed like the scenario and so on.

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:09.570
Okay.

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:16.319
And so now of all the activities you do, you
just mentioned bug bounty, pen tests, courses,

00:06:16.319 --> 00:06:17.500
creating content.

00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:23.510
And I don't think if I missed anything, what
are the activities you enjoy the most?

00:06:23.510 --> 00:06:25.870
I still love the public speaking aspect of
it.

00:06:25.870 --> 00:06:28.500
I still love going to conferences and speaking.

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:32.770
It gives me the chance to like travel to go
to different places and talk to different

00:06:32.770 --> 00:06:35.870
backgrounds and different people.

00:06:35.870 --> 00:06:39.900
And I also still enjoy the bug bounty and
obviously content creation is probably one

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:41.870
of the most enjoyable things.

00:06:41.870 --> 00:06:46.430
I think bug bounties is what I do to get that
like itch of wanting to like do something

00:06:46.430 --> 00:06:51.280
technical like being behind a keyboard, something
with a pen test.

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:55.139
With a pen test, I don't have the freedom
to do whatever I want.

00:06:55.139 --> 00:06:59.550
Pen test is like pen testing an app and getting
it done.

00:06:59.550 --> 00:07:02.901
And then with content, I get the creativity
out.

00:07:02.901 --> 00:07:05.770
I get to get creative and produce some art,
right?

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:07.690
Like I get to create the thumbnails.

00:07:07.690 --> 00:07:09.639
I get to think about the story I want to tell.

00:07:09.639 --> 00:07:14.530
I get to talk about like the things that I
want to talk about and they're very inspirational

00:07:14.530 --> 00:07:16.190
more than anything.

00:07:16.190 --> 00:07:19.949
But that gives me the art that I want to do
because I think that's some form of art.

00:07:19.949 --> 00:07:20.949
You're creating something.

00:07:20.949 --> 00:07:21.949
It's a form of art.

00:07:21.949 --> 00:07:26.100
So I enjoy honestly a lot of the things that
I do.

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:29.350
I can't say what is the one thing that I enjoy
the most to be honest.

00:07:29.350 --> 00:07:30.630
I enjoy them all.

00:07:30.630 --> 00:07:35.590
Yeah, but you actually mentioned I think most
of them which just speaks to people that you

00:07:35.590 --> 00:07:39.370
are in the right place because you enjoy most
if not all the things you do.

00:07:39.370 --> 00:07:42.629
So that sounds really great.

00:07:42.629 --> 00:07:47.700
Yeah, I mean there are times where I wake
up and I don't want to do anything.

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:50.870
I have those days when I wake up and I don't
want to make videos.

00:07:50.870 --> 00:07:54.930
This week has been really hard to record videos
because I just came back from travel last

00:07:54.930 --> 00:07:59.009
week and I'm still getting back into my routine
and I have a bunch of stuff that I have to

00:07:59.009 --> 00:08:00.009
catch up on.

00:08:00.009 --> 00:08:04.320
So I've been pushing video recording all week
and today I have to sit down and record Monday's

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:06.990
video and make sure I have it ready to be
out by Monday.

00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:11.150
I have those days like everybody else but
it's just mostly I've learned how to like

00:08:11.150 --> 00:08:15.940
push myself and also telling myself like,
yeah, if I don't want to make a video today,

00:08:15.940 --> 00:08:18.830
I'll do something else that I have to do on
my to-do list and then get to it later.

00:08:18.830 --> 00:08:21.560
Yeah, yeah, I definitely know what you mean.

00:08:21.560 --> 00:08:27.169
I struggle a lot with coming back to the work
mode after some traveling, after some trips.

00:08:27.169 --> 00:08:32.080
It always takes me so much time and back in
the days when I would work nine to five, I

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:37.301
would just be at work because I had to so
I didn't have this problem.

00:08:37.301 --> 00:08:41.649
And it's different because like when you work
a nine to five, even if you don't perform

00:08:41.649 --> 00:08:44.350
at your best, you still have a guaranteed
paycheck.

00:08:44.350 --> 00:08:45.350
It's okay when you're...

00:08:45.350 --> 00:08:48.970
I'm not saying it's okay like after work but
when you come back, I think people are more

00:08:48.970 --> 00:08:51.769
understanding for the first couple of days
until you get back in the routine.

00:08:51.769 --> 00:08:52.769
It's normal.

00:08:52.769 --> 00:08:56.230
But when you work for yourself, I feel like
it's a little bit of different pressure.

00:08:56.230 --> 00:09:02.920
Yeah, and probably if you work a little bit
less productively for the three days after

00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:08.560
the trip, no one just will not notice because
no one's monitoring close enough.

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:10.670
Absolutely, yeah.

00:09:10.670 --> 00:09:15.520
So being in the back bounty pretty much since
the beginning because I think I can safely

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:21.430
say this and working at HackerOne, how did
you see the industry change over the years?

00:09:21.430 --> 00:09:28.019
It's very cool to see almost like every company
now has a bug bounty program or a VDP at least.

00:09:28.019 --> 00:09:32.200
I see often more companies where I type in
the name and the word security where something

00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:35.040
pops up than I used to 10 years ago.

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:37.990
And then the amount of people that are doing
bug bounties now, it's insane.

00:09:37.990 --> 00:09:42.740
The amount of people that have done bug bounties
or have something for bug bounties, either

00:09:42.740 --> 00:09:47.350
they're content creators because of bug bounties
or because they're pen testers because they

00:09:47.350 --> 00:09:50.780
learned about HackerOne or something, or just
even the people that are freelancing bug bounties

00:09:50.780 --> 00:09:52.440
and it's insane.

00:09:52.440 --> 00:09:54.639
The leaderboard is like, you just look at
it now.

00:09:54.639 --> 00:09:57.280
I used to know almost everybody on the leaderboard
back in the day.

00:09:57.280 --> 00:10:00.470
But now I look at it and I go, I don't even
know who these people are.

00:10:00.470 --> 00:10:04.149
Like there's so many people now, it's hard
to know everybody.

00:10:04.149 --> 00:10:09.580
I think that has been a good shift and I think
the bug bounty community has helped create

00:10:09.580 --> 00:10:12.570
a lot of the tools and the research that's
been done recently.

00:10:12.570 --> 00:10:19.460
You look at somebody from an asset note from
Chopin and Shaw and his team at Asset Note,

00:10:19.460 --> 00:10:22.730
they're crushing research and I'm not saying
they're doing it because of bug bounties,

00:10:22.730 --> 00:10:28.200
but they all come from a bug bounty background
and they use bug bounties as a proving ground.

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:32.620
They find a vulnerability, they see how many
bug bounty programs can they exploit.

00:10:32.620 --> 00:10:36.269
And then when they finish that, you see all
these other bug bounty hunters using things

00:10:36.269 --> 00:10:41.420
like Nukely, who was also created by bug bounty
hunters to go after the targets that they

00:10:41.420 --> 00:10:42.870
may have not had access to.

00:10:42.870 --> 00:10:51.980
I think it's just bug bounties have contributed
to a good large scale of the security posture

00:10:51.980 --> 00:10:56.260
of organizations, whether it's to have a bug
bounty program or they're using tools that

00:10:56.260 --> 00:11:01.000
were created by a bug bounty program or a
bug bounty hack hunter or just a knowledge

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:02.220
that was shared from a bug bounty hunter.

00:11:02.220 --> 00:11:07.910
I think it's just the ecosystem of at least
web application security and ASM has been

00:11:07.910 --> 00:11:14.450
drastically contributed to by bug bounty hunters
from HackerOne, BugCrowd or whatever other

00:11:14.450 --> 00:11:17.520
platform you can think of.

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:22.570
Do you think it was easier a few years ago
because there were bugs everywhere or do you

00:11:22.570 --> 00:11:27.190
think it is easier today because there are
so many resources?

00:11:27.190 --> 00:11:31.970
I honestly think it's both.

00:11:31.970 --> 00:11:37.019
It was easier because less knowledge was shared
back in the day, but the number of targets

00:11:37.019 --> 00:11:38.019
were less.

00:11:38.019 --> 00:11:40.269
It's like a double-edged sword, right?

00:11:40.269 --> 00:11:42.330
It's like you have both sides of this thing.

00:11:42.330 --> 00:11:45.450
Yeah, there was less programs, but less knowledge.

00:11:45.450 --> 00:11:48.459
But now you have more knowledge, but more
programs.

00:11:48.459 --> 00:11:53.680
And honestly, I still see the dumb easy cross-site
scriptings and IDORS and some of these large

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:58.190
organizations that are Fortune 500 or Fortune
100 or whatever you want to call them.

00:11:58.190 --> 00:12:00.839
I still see the same mistakes happening over
and over and over.

00:12:00.839 --> 00:12:03.010
So you can't say there are less bugs.

00:12:03.010 --> 00:12:05.730
I just think there's more competition.

00:12:05.730 --> 00:12:10.079
And honestly, it's disappointing a little
bit if you ask me because I think a lot of

00:12:10.079 --> 00:12:15.120
these bug bounty hunters have the wrong idea
of automation and recon and everyone's focused

00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:16.120
on the wrong things.

00:12:16.120 --> 00:12:21.079
And that's what I'm finding vulnerabilities
and I think it gives the impression that there

00:12:21.079 --> 00:12:22.139
are less bugs to be found.

00:12:22.139 --> 00:12:25.930
But if there is less bugs to be found, how
are these companies paying millions of dollars

00:12:25.930 --> 00:12:26.959
every month?

00:12:26.959 --> 00:12:33.860
Yeah, and especially like life hacking events
show us that there are bugs everywhere.

00:12:33.860 --> 00:12:38.450
But what wrong things do you mean here that
hunters focus on?

00:12:38.450 --> 00:12:45.430
So I think a lot of the newer hunters think
like I have this super secret tool on my machine

00:12:45.430 --> 00:12:49.250
that I launch a company name and it just goes
bugs, bugs, bugs, bugs.

00:12:49.250 --> 00:12:51.060
It's not like that.

00:12:51.060 --> 00:12:52.650
You know, you have things like nuclear that
does that.

00:12:52.650 --> 00:12:56.190
I know people that have like slack integrations
and webhooks that does that.

00:12:56.190 --> 00:12:57.190
Right.

00:12:57.190 --> 00:13:02.070
But I want to say I don't want to give it
a percentage, but a large number of vulnerabilities

00:13:02.070 --> 00:13:06.139
that come out that are good, that are paid
high enough.

00:13:06.139 --> 00:13:07.660
It takes a lot of manual work.

00:13:07.660 --> 00:13:13.760
They may have found something that's very
cool or very unique through recon, but the

00:13:13.760 --> 00:13:17.639
exploitation is completely manual and takes
research.

00:13:17.639 --> 00:13:19.820
You're finding out what this application is.

00:13:19.820 --> 00:13:21.010
Is it open sourced?

00:13:21.010 --> 00:13:22.620
Is there a similar data on this thing?

00:13:22.620 --> 00:13:25.400
Is there a similar input that we can find
as a documentation?

00:13:25.400 --> 00:13:26.480
Right.

00:13:26.480 --> 00:13:30.430
And I think a lot of hackers don't want to
spend that time because let's be honest, it

00:13:30.430 --> 00:13:31.430
looks boring.

00:13:31.430 --> 00:13:32.430
It doesn't look fun.

00:13:32.430 --> 00:13:34.149
It's a lot of hours to be spent.

00:13:34.149 --> 00:13:35.880
But if you have the passion, it's a different
story.

00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:40.600
I don't mind digging into the same program
for two weeks at a time until I find a bug

00:13:40.600 --> 00:13:45.470
because 14 days and I spend and I make, I
don't know, let's say $20,000.

00:13:45.470 --> 00:13:47.000
That's still more than a job.

00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:49.540
I would have made in 18 days, right?

00:13:49.540 --> 00:13:50.540
Yeah.

00:13:50.540 --> 00:13:51.540
So the return is there.

00:13:51.540 --> 00:13:53.750
It's just people want instant gratification.

00:13:53.750 --> 00:13:55.269
I think it's a curse of the internet.

00:13:55.269 --> 00:13:56.800
Everyone wants things quickly like this.

00:13:56.800 --> 00:13:58.400
They want the bounty tomorrow.

00:13:58.400 --> 00:13:59.400
I started today.

00:13:59.400 --> 00:14:00.400
I must get a bounty tomorrow.

00:14:00.400 --> 00:14:01.400
And that's not the case.

00:14:01.400 --> 00:14:05.630
You don't, you know, there's only a few amount
of people that have the overnight success.

00:14:05.630 --> 00:14:08.970
And unfortunately, it doesn't happen that
often in any industry.

00:14:08.970 --> 00:14:09.970
Yeah.

00:14:09.970 --> 00:14:10.970
Yeah.

00:14:10.970 --> 00:14:16.620
It's even if we sometimes have these stories
that we find a bug very quickly, there are

