00:27:12.279 --> 00:27:16.500
It would say SQL injection in this file.

00:27:16.500 --> 00:27:19.820
And then I would go and look for the file
and see if I can exploit it myself.

00:27:19.820 --> 00:27:24.360
When you use these tools, even if you're learning,
you get maybe just become reliant on them.

00:27:24.360 --> 00:27:26.149
It just takes a fun out of it.

00:27:26.149 --> 00:27:31.250
I'm very much like very, very, very, like
black or white.

00:27:31.250 --> 00:27:33.179
Like it's, I'm either on this side or on that
side.

00:27:33.179 --> 00:27:35.690
Either I use a lot of tools or I don't use
tools at all.

00:27:35.690 --> 00:27:40.970
There's no, I can't find the right balance,
but I just feel like using a lot of tools

00:27:40.970 --> 00:27:43.740
makes me just lazy and less productive.

00:27:43.740 --> 00:27:45.370
And I rely on them too much.

00:27:45.370 --> 00:27:46.370
So I've cut it down.

00:27:46.370 --> 00:27:50.800
And, you know, even like with looking at a
webpage, I try to use the network tab more

00:27:50.800 --> 00:27:51.800
than anything.

00:27:51.800 --> 00:27:55.240
And then I switch over to Burp suite when
I want to like plus something in a repeater,

00:27:55.240 --> 00:27:56.240
for example.

00:27:56.240 --> 00:27:57.240
Yeah.

00:27:57.240 --> 00:28:02.190
And actually they just added to Chrome the
ability to modify response headers in network

00:28:02.190 --> 00:28:04.470
dev tools, which is also great.

00:28:04.470 --> 00:28:06.250
I don't know if you need Burp anymore.

00:28:06.250 --> 00:28:11.429
I mean, for me, at least at this point, but
it's just, I think you got to learn how to

00:28:11.429 --> 00:28:16.990
do these things before you jump onto tools,
to be honest.

00:28:16.990 --> 00:28:18.049
That's just me.

00:28:18.049 --> 00:28:24.360
I just think there is a, I'm trying to make
sure the advice that I give people makes sense.

00:28:24.360 --> 00:28:27.429
They, like I'm doing the things that I give
advice to.

00:28:27.429 --> 00:28:31.320
So if I'm not living by the advice that I
give people, then why am I giving this advice

00:28:31.320 --> 00:28:32.320
to them?

00:28:32.320 --> 00:28:33.320
Right.

00:28:33.320 --> 00:28:38.390
So if I tell you to stop doing recon, if I
don't stop doing it myself, then I'm a hypocrite.

00:28:38.390 --> 00:28:39.390
Yeah.

00:28:39.390 --> 00:28:44.900
And also like regarding automations and everything,
also often people ask me, how do you, what

00:28:44.900 --> 00:28:46.230
should they automate?

00:28:46.230 --> 00:28:50.929
My response is just automate something that
you are just tired of doing manually.

00:28:50.929 --> 00:28:54.620
Your, I think your asset discovery stuff should
be automated.

00:28:54.620 --> 00:29:01.150
For example, you should let a tool, for example,
look for like a sub-lister.

00:29:01.150 --> 00:29:02.490
You should do that.

00:29:02.490 --> 00:29:07.809
Your information gathering should be something
like sub-lister, but you don't need to automate.

00:29:07.809 --> 00:29:13.950
I know people that are like sharing things
like, oh, do this one-liner to find cross-site

00:29:13.950 --> 00:29:14.950
scriptings.

00:29:14.950 --> 00:29:20.240
Why, why, why, or our SQL, why are we feeding
every endpoint to a SQL map?

00:29:20.240 --> 00:29:21.820
What's the point in that, right?

00:29:21.820 --> 00:29:25.260
That's an easy way to get banned with a WAF,
for example.

00:29:25.260 --> 00:29:27.520
It's, I get that people want to be productive.

00:29:27.520 --> 00:29:31.470
There's a difference between being productive
and just being straight up lazy.

00:29:31.470 --> 00:29:32.529
Be productive, be efficient.

00:29:32.529 --> 00:29:37.580
You should use the tools to make you faster,
not to make you money, if that makes sense.

00:29:37.580 --> 00:29:38.580
Make you more efficient.

00:29:38.580 --> 00:29:41.820
So the things that, like Nucleus is a good
example of it.

00:29:41.820 --> 00:29:47.179
You can use, you can still make money by running
Nucleus templates and you can make some money,

00:29:47.179 --> 00:29:50.720
but you can make more money if you have your
own Nucleus templates that you have found,

00:29:50.720 --> 00:29:53.100
that you've eventually loaded into Nucleus.

00:29:53.100 --> 00:29:54.950
I don't use Nucleus at all.

00:29:54.950 --> 00:30:00.490
I don't like, it's not that I don't like Nucleus,
it's just, it just made me purely lazy doing

00:30:00.490 --> 00:30:03.090
that kind of automation and there's so many
people that are doing Nucleus now.

00:30:03.090 --> 00:30:05.450
You gotta stand out somehow.

00:30:05.450 --> 00:30:13.399
Yeah, and so we are, we've talked a bit about
what people overrate, that they overrate recon

00:30:13.399 --> 00:30:14.990
and using tools.

00:30:14.990 --> 00:30:20.390
On the other hand, as a content creator, you
also give people a lot of tips and probably

00:30:20.390 --> 00:30:25.570
sometimes you see that people don't appreciate
some tips as they should.

00:30:25.570 --> 00:30:27.279
They don't ask correct questions.

00:30:27.279 --> 00:30:31.460
What do you think are things that people don't
appreciate?

00:30:31.460 --> 00:30:35.090
So one of the things that I made a comment
in one of my videos is a lot of people are

00:30:35.090 --> 00:30:40.549
more worried about the what they use.

00:30:40.549 --> 00:30:49.720
So if you look, anytime somebody posts something
on let's say on Twitter, I make a post that

00:30:49.720 --> 00:30:53.130
says, I just had a cross-site scripting in
Google.

00:30:53.130 --> 00:30:57.510
Do you know what the number one question is
that's gonna be responded to that tweet?

00:30:57.510 --> 00:30:59.429
No idea.

00:30:59.429 --> 00:31:00.630
What's the payload you used?

00:31:00.630 --> 00:31:02.460
Of course, yeah.

00:31:02.460 --> 00:31:03.460
Right?

00:31:03.460 --> 00:31:07.639
Why is the question, what's the payload you
used and not how did you think of that?

00:31:07.639 --> 00:31:08.639
Yeah, yeah.

00:31:08.639 --> 00:31:10.510
Because the what doesn't fucking matter.

00:31:10.510 --> 00:31:12.390
You can use a cross-site scripting payload.

00:31:12.390 --> 00:31:15.529
It's not, you know, you can make your own
payload if you understand HTML and JavaScript.

00:31:15.529 --> 00:31:20.190
I guess it's usually something easy or it's
some cool bypass that you could figure out

00:31:20.190 --> 00:31:23.429
after a few steps of like, you know, fuzzing
the application, right?

00:31:23.429 --> 00:31:27.549
But asking somebody like, why did you think
this cross-site scripting was gonna work?

00:31:27.549 --> 00:31:30.429
Or why do you think the CSP bypass was gonna
work?

00:31:30.429 --> 00:31:31.429
Right?

00:31:31.429 --> 00:31:34.750
Or why did you think there's, why did you
think there's an RCE versus like how did you

00:31:34.750 --> 00:31:35.809
exploit this RCE?

00:31:35.809 --> 00:31:40.690
Yeah, that's a very good, very good tip.

00:31:40.690 --> 00:31:44.820
People never ask this, never ask me this as
well.

00:31:44.820 --> 00:31:46.940
Yeah, it's always what did you use?

00:31:46.940 --> 00:31:48.139
What did you, what did you do?

00:31:48.139 --> 00:31:49.139
Like what payload?

00:31:49.139 --> 00:31:50.139
What's the payload?

00:31:50.139 --> 00:31:51.139
What's the payload?

00:31:51.139 --> 00:31:52.139
I'm like, stop asking for the payload, dude.

00:31:52.139 --> 00:31:53.190
Ask me how I thought about this.

00:31:53.190 --> 00:31:55.460
You know, the payload doesn't matter.

00:31:55.460 --> 00:31:59.370
It's my half, nine out of 10 times my payload
is a genetic cross-site scripting payload

00:31:59.370 --> 00:32:02.440
or a genetic blind cross-site scripting payload.

00:32:02.440 --> 00:32:05.840
But there is a closing tag before that everybody
else missed, right?

00:32:05.840 --> 00:32:10.059
I skipped out of some tag that I knew was
gonna be there, but you're not asking me how

00:32:10.059 --> 00:32:11.590
I knew this, right?

00:32:11.590 --> 00:32:16.370
So this, I think the biggest thing is like,
if you're watching this, if you wanna become

00:32:16.370 --> 00:32:22.200
a good bug bounty hunter, shifting the mentality
of like what tools they used and what payload

00:32:22.200 --> 00:32:26.240
they used to how did they use that tool or
how did they think about this?

00:32:26.240 --> 00:32:29.630
What is the thought process to get here is
more important.

00:32:29.630 --> 00:32:30.850
That's one of the biggest ones.

00:32:30.850 --> 00:32:33.360
And I know people who just copy paste payloads.

00:32:33.360 --> 00:32:38.580
I copy paste my own payloads, but you don't
see me going like RCE payload and then like,

00:32:38.580 --> 00:32:43.570
bam, bam, throwing them in every single, you
know, box that I see.

00:32:43.570 --> 00:32:48.590
Yeah, and especially that, as you said, most
of payloads are just the, for me, most of

00:32:48.590 --> 00:32:51.289
XSS were just the very basic payloads.

00:32:51.289 --> 00:32:58.649
And the other times they are, they look complex
for someone, but they actually are very simple

00:32:58.649 --> 00:33:03.120
if you follow the thought process, if you
see the filters that were there.

00:33:03.120 --> 00:33:08.330
And also those that the complex payload often
makes absolutely no sense in any other target.

00:33:08.330 --> 00:33:11.929
So copying and pasting, it just won't help
you in anything.

00:33:11.929 --> 00:33:17.510
And actually understanding what happened,
this is what will give people bucks in other

00:33:17.510 --> 00:33:18.510
applications.

00:33:18.510 --> 00:33:24.159
And when I do my, you know, the live recon
streams that I do, I interview people.

00:33:24.159 --> 00:33:28.019
One of the most common questions people ask
is like, can you ask him what tools this person

00:33:28.019 --> 00:33:30.179
uses for subdomain enumeration?

00:33:30.179 --> 00:33:33.950
I'm like, you understand, it doesn't really
probably matter.

00:33:33.950 --> 00:33:40.340
Like all these tools, so you think about it,
like SubLister, Find Domain, I don't know,

00:33:40.340 --> 00:33:45.120
X, Y, and Z, all these different ones, Amass,
Amass is kind of different, but let's just

00:33:45.120 --> 00:33:47.389
put it in there for like the subdomain enumeration,
right?

00:33:47.389 --> 00:33:49.090
They all have the same thing in common.

00:33:49.090 --> 00:33:50.740
They hit the same APIs.

00:33:50.740 --> 00:33:52.600
You have to give it the API key.

00:33:52.600 --> 00:33:56.570
They all mostly hit the same sources and they
gave you similar data.

00:33:56.570 --> 00:34:01.120
Some of them do better, some of them do more
because their searching queries are different,

00:34:01.120 --> 00:34:02.120
right?

00:34:02.120 --> 00:34:03.710
Everyone asks, what tool do you use?

00:34:03.710 --> 00:34:07.020
But no one's asking, what do you do with this
data next?

00:34:07.020 --> 00:34:08.020
Most people don't ask that.

00:34:08.020 --> 00:34:11.780
They just think like, oh, you must have this
like crazy configuration that gets you data.

00:34:11.780 --> 00:34:17.649
Sure, I'll give you the same data, but are
you gonna be able to find the same bugs?

00:34:17.649 --> 00:34:19.389
Yeah, yeah, it's very, very common.

00:34:19.389 --> 00:34:26.159
I think it also kind of, the reason for this,
and I also had this very feeling before I

00:34:26.159 --> 00:34:32.149
started hunting that to find a bug, I need
to find a subdomain or a target that nobody

00:34:32.149 --> 00:34:33.149
else finds.

00:34:33.149 --> 00:34:38.760
And I think the reason for this, the reason
beginners ask this question too much is that

00:34:38.760 --> 00:34:45.260
they are not confident enough that they can
find a bug in a target that anybody else has

00:34:45.260 --> 00:34:46.470
hacked.

00:34:46.470 --> 00:34:52.909
And we just see over and over and over again
that everyone skips bugs because we just see

00:34:52.909 --> 00:34:59.320
in the recent live hacking events, you said
it was a target, live hacking events, for

00:34:59.320 --> 00:35:04.200
those who didn't know are, actually, maybe
you can tell people what are live hacking

00:35:04.200 --> 00:35:05.830
events, you know better than me.

00:35:05.830 --> 00:35:09.940
So the live hacking events are, HackerOne
has been doing this for years, I think other

00:35:09.940 --> 00:35:13.710
companies do them now too, but HackerOne is
like live hacking events in a city.

00:35:13.710 --> 00:35:16.800
They pick a random city, this last one was
in London.

00:35:16.800 --> 00:35:21.520
They usually pick one target, sometimes two
or three, depending on the event.

00:35:21.520 --> 00:35:25.490
But usually it's like one or two companies,
they fly everybody in, they put us in a room,

00:35:25.490 --> 00:35:26.490
we hack.

00:35:26.490 --> 00:35:30.040
The cool thing about the bug bounty events
with the live hacking events is you have direct

00:35:30.040 --> 00:35:34.579
access to the triage team, you have direct
access to the engineering team.

00:35:34.579 --> 00:35:38.990
If I have to make an assumption, I could go
up to an engineer and validate that assumption

00:35:38.990 --> 00:35:43.470
or show him a possible route or whatever that
is, you have access to them.

00:35:43.470 --> 00:35:45.840
This last one is public knowledge, it was
Zoom and Salesforce.

00:35:45.840 --> 00:35:50.720
Both of them were on the local HackerOne chair,
so we can confidently talk about Zoom and

00:35:50.720 --> 00:35:55.579
Salesforce being the targets, which these
are multi-billion dollar company maybe at

00:35:55.579 --> 00:36:00.280
this point in revenue, but also the security
teams they have are huge.

00:36:00.280 --> 00:36:06.520
Chances are people have hacked on Zoom a bunch
of times or Zoom has done a bunch of pen tests,

00:36:06.520 --> 00:36:09.080
there's one of the most commonly used tools
out there.

00:36:09.080 --> 00:36:16.200
But people still found a ton of vulnerability
because not only they have new things that

00:36:16.200 --> 00:36:20.550
are coming out, they're developing new features,
they give us access to and so on, but it's

00:36:20.550 --> 00:36:23.630
just the fact that people have their pressure,
you have to find bugs, this is the target,

00:36:23.630 --> 00:36:26.490
this is what it is, you have to look for bugs
in them.

00:36:26.490 --> 00:36:31.470
The comment that you made about the assumption
of finding a subdomain to be first to find

00:36:31.470 --> 00:36:35.730
a vulnerability in, that assumption works.

00:36:35.730 --> 00:36:41.890
There's a payout in finding a subdomain, being
the first to find it in a company like Uber,

00:36:41.890 --> 00:36:44.660
Yahoo, big large organizations that are public.

00:36:44.660 --> 00:36:48.500
It helps when you're first to find a subdomain,
because if they deploy something for testing

00:36:48.500 --> 00:36:52.450
purposes and you're the first to find it as
it goes up, then you're probably going to

00:36:52.450 --> 00:36:56.480
be the first to find a vulnerability, but
that's not the only way to find a vulnerability

00:36:56.480 --> 00:37:00.859
and these life hacking events are for sure
a good example of showing that.

00:37:00.859 --> 00:37:06.630
Yeah, and speaking of life hacking events,
we have a question from Johan Carson from

00:37:06.630 --> 00:37:07.950
Twitter.

00:37:07.950 --> 00:37:13.030
How do you create teams for life hacking events?

00:37:13.030 --> 00:37:17.500
I think the life hacking events, the teams
that are already created is just based on

00:37:17.500 --> 00:37:18.500
everyone that's already been friends.

00:37:18.500 --> 00:37:23.040
A lot of the same people keep on collaborating
over and over and over again.

00:37:23.040 --> 00:37:27.119
I don't usually have a team, unfortunately,
or fortunately for me.

00:37:27.119 --> 00:37:33.840
I try to team up with people, but I think
it's mostly I end up hacking with people that

00:37:33.840 --> 00:37:36.170
I'm close with most of the time.

00:37:36.170 --> 00:37:41.030
And if they're not there or if that's not
the case, just you bump shoulders with people.

00:37:41.030 --> 00:37:45.800
So in my last video when I talked about Zoom,
I worked with Alex Chapman and Shmuel.

00:37:45.800 --> 00:37:50.950
I've known Alex for years, great guy, but
Shmuel I had never met in my life ever.

00:37:50.950 --> 00:37:52.630
I never even knew who he was.

00:37:52.630 --> 00:37:54.840
We've never talked at all online.

00:37:54.840 --> 00:37:58.780
And we're in the lobby waiting for the car
to take us to the event.

00:37:58.780 --> 00:38:00.630
He walks up to me and goes, hey, I'm Shmuel,
what's your name?

00:38:00.630 --> 00:38:01.630
And I'm like, oh, hey, I'm Ben.

00:38:01.630 --> 00:38:02.630
You know, I'm the homestick on the platform.

00:38:02.630 --> 00:38:04.030
Oh yeah, I've seen your blah, blah, blah.

00:38:04.030 --> 00:38:05.760
And what are you doing here?

00:38:05.760 --> 00:38:09.060
And then I tell him like, oh yeah, I've got
this bug, but I can't figure it out.

00:38:09.060 --> 00:38:11.230
He goes, oh, we can for sure do this one thing
to it.

00:38:11.230 --> 00:38:12.450
And I'm like, no way.

00:38:12.450 --> 00:38:16.170
And then 16 hours later, I'm sitting here,
I'm high-fiving him a million times.

00:38:16.170 --> 00:38:18.440
Like, yeah, yeah, we did it, we did it.

00:38:18.440 --> 00:38:19.540
16 hours later, right?

00:38:19.540 --> 00:38:24.340
It's just, it's a cool thing to be at these
events because just bumping to someone in

00:38:24.340 --> 00:38:30.000
the lobby or at the event where you can just
go, hey, I'm so-and-so, what's up?

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:34.200
Just easier and more natural if you have that
to go walk up to people.

00:38:34.200 --> 00:38:35.970
It makes it just easier to collaborate.

00:38:35.970 --> 00:38:37.800
And, you know, honestly, teams aren't the
only way.

00:38:37.800 --> 00:38:41.101
I know people that just collaborate with anybody
and there's a collab channel that you can

00:38:41.101 --> 00:38:42.240
work with.

00:38:42.240 --> 00:38:51.470
So do you mostly team up with either your
friends or someone you bump up at the event

00:38:51.470 --> 00:38:54.190
for like the whole event you collaborate together?

00:38:54.190 --> 00:39:00.230
Or is it more like with, in this case, to
exploit a bug that you found?

00:39:00.230 --> 00:39:03.300
I think, so I've only been to a couple of
it.

00:39:03.300 --> 00:39:06.580
So I used to work at HackerOne and I used
to go to the events as an employee so I couldn't

00:39:06.580 --> 00:39:07.980
participate a lot of times.

00:39:07.980 --> 00:39:10.230
This is my third or fourth event.

00:39:10.230 --> 00:39:13.750
The first one I went to, I think, was Vegas
last year.

00:39:13.750 --> 00:39:15.090
No, Denver last year.

00:39:15.090 --> 00:39:19.020
That one, I think, Justin and I, Renovator,
we were just like, hey, let's team up for

00:39:19.020 --> 00:39:20.220
this event together and hack.

00:39:20.220 --> 00:39:22.700
We teamed up, made some money and that was
it.

00:39:22.700 --> 00:39:24.780
But we didn't find a whole lot of stuff.

00:39:24.780 --> 00:39:28.109
It was kind of like defeated the purpose,
but it was an experience I wanted to do together,

00:39:28.109 --> 00:39:29.109
right?

00:39:29.109 --> 00:39:30.790
And then the second event was Vegas.

00:39:30.790 --> 00:39:31.790
I work with Ziad a lot.

00:39:31.790 --> 00:39:32.980
He's one of my good friends.

00:39:32.980 --> 00:39:35.730
Him and I were just, we haven't seen each
other for years.

00:39:35.730 --> 00:39:36.730
Hey, let's team up.

00:39:36.730 --> 00:39:38.560
We get there and neither one of us had bugs.

00:39:38.560 --> 00:39:42.550
The night before the event, we set up all
night hacking and we ended up finding a ton

00:39:42.550 --> 00:39:43.550
of bug.

00:39:43.550 --> 00:39:44.550
That kind of worked out.

00:39:44.550 --> 00:39:47.099
It wasn't like we agreed to be a team, but
it was just like, we're friends.

00:39:47.099 --> 00:39:50.290
I would rather work with him than anybody
else because I trust the guy, I know him.

00:39:50.290 --> 00:39:52.400
It just makes it more fun.

00:39:52.400 --> 00:39:55.130
And then in LA, I went and did solo.

00:39:55.130 --> 00:39:56.130
They didn't find anything.

00:39:56.130 --> 00:39:57.130
It was way over my head.

00:39:57.130 --> 00:40:02.290
And then this event, I went in solo, but I
collaborated with people like Eric.

00:40:02.290 --> 00:40:07.230
I collaborated with, what's his name?

00:40:07.230 --> 00:40:09.490
I just mentioned Shmuel and Alex Chapman.

00:40:09.490 --> 00:40:14.089
And it was just more of a, I found someone
that has interests like I do or knows the

00:40:14.089 --> 00:40:17.170
things that I do or thinks we can do something
together.

00:40:17.170 --> 00:40:21.290
Screw it, let's team up and let's see where
it goes.

00:40:21.290 --> 00:40:26.849
And the other question from Johan was, so
when you're collaborating together, do you

