I'm not a web3 expert by any means.

00:52:39.000 --> 00:52:43.010
I just made a lot of web3 content, thanks
to Halborn.

00:52:43.010 --> 00:52:48.030
I was exposed to a lot of web3 things, but
it's way over my head, but honestly, if you

00:52:48.030 --> 00:52:49.990
have, I think I know what the question is.

00:52:49.990 --> 00:52:50.990
I can guess what it is.

00:52:50.990 --> 00:52:57.010
Let me see if I can answer without you asking,
but honestly, if you have an interest in developing

00:52:57.010 --> 00:53:01.730
and you have a good understanding of programming
languages and you're okay with learning and

00:53:01.730 --> 00:53:06.329
doing source code review, web3 is very fun
and very lucrative.

00:53:06.329 --> 00:53:07.730
You can make a lot of money.

00:53:07.730 --> 00:53:11.990
If you're looking into getting started with
web3, which I think is probably the question,

00:53:11.990 --> 00:53:15.520
honestly, the easiest way I can- There's actually
no follow-up question to this.

00:53:15.520 --> 00:53:16.520
Oh, okay.

00:53:16.520 --> 00:53:18.569
Well, I was going to say, if people want to
get into web3, I get a lot of questions because

00:53:18.569 --> 00:53:20.560
of the content that I made with Halborn.

00:53:20.560 --> 00:53:24.400
If you want to get into web3, honestly, Zillow
has some really good resources.

00:53:24.400 --> 00:53:27.710
I know Halborn themselves have a lot of good
resources, and I think they have a CTF on

00:53:27.710 --> 00:53:31.670
their GitHub and they have a Zion, which is
pretty, really dope.

00:53:31.670 --> 00:53:36.970
Now, you have all the tools you need for it
that I recommend people using, but outside

00:53:36.970 --> 00:53:40.849
of that, the biggest thing is just, it's like
anything else.

00:53:40.849 --> 00:53:41.849
It's like doing it.

00:53:41.849 --> 00:53:49.510
You have to be okay with sitting in, sitting
down and just reviewing source code and having

00:53:49.510 --> 00:53:54.430
analogies and hunches that you're going to
go on and testing them out.

00:53:54.430 --> 00:53:56.540
Resource-wise, I don't have a lot of resources.

00:53:56.540 --> 00:54:00.480
Go watch my videos that I've created.

00:54:00.480 --> 00:54:03.980
HackerMate is a really good hacker out of,
I want to say Spain or Portugal.

00:54:03.980 --> 00:54:05.020
I think he's in Spain.

00:54:05.020 --> 00:54:06.650
He has some really good content.

00:54:06.650 --> 00:54:08.309
I would say follow him as well.

00:54:08.309 --> 00:54:10.049
Farron is really good.

00:54:10.049 --> 00:54:11.049
I follow him on Twitter.

00:54:11.049 --> 00:54:12.329
He has some good resources.

00:54:12.329 --> 00:54:17.640
But I know a lot of the Web3 stuff with Immunify
also as well.

00:54:17.640 --> 00:54:21.119
I have good resources and it's a lot of source
code review.

00:54:21.119 --> 00:54:23.520
Yeah, okay.

00:54:23.520 --> 00:54:28.210
Let's skip to the content creation side of
things.

00:54:28.210 --> 00:54:34.940
Recently, there has been a tweet saying that
top bug bounty hunters don't share their methodologies.

00:54:34.940 --> 00:54:40.510
You are a top bug bounty hunter and you share
a lot of methodologies in your videos since

00:54:40.510 --> 00:54:41.570
years.

00:54:41.570 --> 00:54:44.900
So what's your opinion about that tweet?

00:54:44.900 --> 00:54:49.340
I think people want to have handouts.

00:54:49.340 --> 00:54:55.440
You can't say people aren't sharing because
if people weren't sharing, then how did nuclear

00:54:55.440 --> 00:54:56.440
templates exist?

00:54:56.440 --> 00:54:58.490
Why would they have thousands of nuclear templates?

00:54:58.490 --> 00:55:01.690
If people didn't share, why is there so...

00:55:01.690 --> 00:55:05.700
Why does NahumCon exist if people didn't share?

00:55:05.700 --> 00:55:06.869
Right?

00:55:06.869 --> 00:55:13.619
You see all these top hackers, RhinoRaider,
Shubbz, ZLZ, all these different people that

00:55:13.619 --> 00:55:15.390
come from NahumCon and speak to Jason Haddix.

00:55:15.390 --> 00:55:20.430
If those didn't exist, then what are people
talking about?

00:55:20.430 --> 00:55:24.180
There's a difference between people sharing
their knowledge and people sharing outright

00:55:24.180 --> 00:55:25.799
how they make their money.

00:55:25.799 --> 00:55:30.839
You don't see Apple putting all their blueprints
of their devices online.

00:55:30.839 --> 00:55:32.160
They tell you how they do marketing.

00:55:32.160 --> 00:55:34.390
They show you what they're doing or they show
you what the device does.

00:55:34.390 --> 00:55:37.380
But I think there's a difference between handouts
and making money.

00:55:37.380 --> 00:55:42.319
I think the bug bounty community is one of
the most toxic communities out there in some

00:55:42.319 --> 00:55:45.880
senses, but people are very welcoming and
people are quick to share.

00:55:45.880 --> 00:55:50.640
If you're not a dick and if you're nice to
people and you aren't begging for things,

00:55:50.640 --> 00:55:52.030
people are quick to help you out.

00:55:52.030 --> 00:55:56.839
And I'm one of the people that was on that
side of it for a very long time of asking

00:55:56.839 --> 00:55:57.839
people for help.

00:55:57.839 --> 00:56:02.030
If you go to my tweets from back in the day,
you will see me asking, what's a host letter

00:56:02.030 --> 00:56:03.030
injection?

00:56:03.030 --> 00:56:04.030
What's a parameter pollution?

00:56:04.030 --> 00:56:05.030
Is there anything that I didn't understand
back then?

00:56:05.030 --> 00:56:09.059
And people were messaging me in DMs or replying
to me and responding.

00:56:09.059 --> 00:56:11.420
And that was in 2014.

00:56:11.420 --> 00:56:15.120
Yeah, but the key is asking a good question
as well.

00:56:15.120 --> 00:56:20.289
Because a lot of people are just asking either
a very generic question, which suggests they

00:56:20.289 --> 00:56:25.810
invested no time into before asking the question.

00:56:25.810 --> 00:56:28.700
And then there's just no answer.

00:56:28.700 --> 00:56:30.789
For me as well, I also get a lot of messages.

00:56:30.789 --> 00:56:35.680
I don't respond to a lot of messages because
they are like, hi, Greg, how are you doing?

00:56:35.680 --> 00:56:37.740
Or very generic, how do you start?

00:56:37.740 --> 00:56:39.900
Blah, blah, blah, things like this.

00:56:39.900 --> 00:56:46.270
But when I see that someone invested some
time before writing to me, I would just feel

00:56:46.270 --> 00:56:48.720
bad not replying to them.

00:56:48.720 --> 00:56:54.970
And I know, again, from being on the other
side that other people help as well.

00:56:54.970 --> 00:56:56.860
So I think there's a couple of things.

00:56:56.860 --> 00:56:57.860
I get the same.

00:56:57.860 --> 00:56:59.910
I get a lot of questions from people coming
in.

00:56:59.910 --> 00:57:02.770
And it's easy to tell who's trying and who's
not trying.

00:57:02.770 --> 00:57:07.670
I get that people want to say, hey, how are
you doing as a first message because you don't

00:57:07.670 --> 00:57:11.030
want to outright just ask someone for a favor,
right?

00:57:11.030 --> 00:57:12.030
I get that.

00:57:12.030 --> 00:57:13.940
But you can say, hey, how are you doing?

00:57:13.940 --> 00:57:15.200
But you want something.

00:57:15.200 --> 00:57:18.020
You're not just messaging me to just ask me
how I'm doing.

00:57:18.020 --> 00:57:19.830
So learn how to ask a question.

00:57:19.830 --> 00:57:23.260
My rule of thumb for everybody that's watching
this is if you want to ask a question from

00:57:23.260 --> 00:57:25.770
anybody, hey, how are you doing?

00:57:25.770 --> 00:57:27.049
Or hey, hello, how's it going?

00:57:27.049 --> 00:57:28.990
Whatever you want to say, greet them, obviously.

00:57:28.990 --> 00:57:31.470
And then say, hey, I'm working on X.

00:57:31.470 --> 00:57:33.970
I'm struggling with Y.

00:57:33.970 --> 00:57:35.470
I've tried these things.

00:57:35.470 --> 00:57:39.530
I was wondering if you know anything about
this thing or how I could proceed.

00:57:39.530 --> 00:57:44.280
I've had a lot of cases of people just saying,
hey, how do you export XSS?

00:57:44.280 --> 00:57:45.380
Go look at Website Academy.

00:57:45.380 --> 00:57:49.550
I'm happy to help you, but it's just I can't
help everybody like that, right?

00:57:49.550 --> 00:57:54.640
And I've had some really cool ones on Discord
that people have messaged me like, hey, I

00:57:54.640 --> 00:57:55.910
have this SSRF on this thing.

00:57:55.910 --> 00:57:57.790
I've tried these things.

00:57:57.790 --> 00:57:58.790
Can you help me?

00:57:58.790 --> 00:57:59.839
Or they go, I have an SSRF I can't export.

00:57:59.839 --> 00:58:00.839
Can you help me?

00:58:00.839 --> 00:58:02.589
I go, OK, I want to see the SSRF.

00:58:02.589 --> 00:58:04.190
So I ask them, what have you tried?

00:58:04.190 --> 00:58:07.900
But when they tell me what they have tried,
I go, oh, I know what to exactly answer for

00:58:07.900 --> 00:58:08.900
you.

00:58:08.900 --> 00:58:12.140
It makes my life easier when I have other
people that are asking me for the same thing.

00:58:12.140 --> 00:58:17.270
So learning how to ask a question is one thing,
but also people just look at the preview of

00:58:17.270 --> 00:58:18.270
your message.

00:58:18.270 --> 00:58:21.500
When the message just says, hey, I'm going
to think you're a bot, because there's a lot

00:58:21.500 --> 00:58:23.240
of bots on Twitter, right?

00:58:23.240 --> 00:58:24.569
So stand out with your question.

00:58:24.569 --> 00:58:25.910
Hey, I'm working on X.

00:58:25.910 --> 00:58:27.730
I was wondering why.

00:58:27.730 --> 00:58:33.630
And I'm curious to see how many people are
going to do this now after watching this episode.

00:58:33.630 --> 00:58:36.589
Yeah, true.

00:58:36.589 --> 00:58:40.390
What is your motivation for creating content?

00:58:40.390 --> 00:58:45.500
I think like when I was doing Bark Brownies
back in 2013, in 2014, there wasn't a lot

00:58:45.500 --> 00:58:47.320
of resources.

00:58:47.320 --> 00:58:50.950
There wasn't anybody sharing any of their
experience.

00:58:50.950 --> 00:58:52.780
I wish somebody was there to do these things.

00:58:52.780 --> 00:58:55.289
And I think it's something that I wish I had.

00:58:55.289 --> 00:58:57.190
And I want to be that person for a lot of
people.

00:58:57.190 --> 00:59:01.920
And I want, honestly, I think hacking changed
my life in a really drastic way.

00:59:01.920 --> 00:59:03.369
In a very huge way.

00:59:03.369 --> 00:59:08.220
Then I think a lot of, if I did it, more people
can definitely do it too.

00:59:08.220 --> 00:59:16.020
Okay, and how do you now organize your time
between all the different projects and hacking

00:59:16.020 --> 00:59:17.760
that you do?

00:59:17.760 --> 00:59:20.609
I think it's just mostly, I used to do like
blogs.

00:59:20.609 --> 00:59:22.650
I would do like this hour, I'm going to do
content.

00:59:22.650 --> 00:59:23.730
This hour, I'm going to hack.

00:59:23.730 --> 00:59:27.960
Then I realized that context switching doesn't
help, especially when you do something creative.

00:59:27.960 --> 00:59:32.780
So now I have days about my content, I have
days that are my hacking days.

00:59:32.780 --> 00:59:35.799
I have days that are like my more chill days.

00:59:35.799 --> 00:59:38.880
It's my days that I make thumbnails or whatever
else.

00:59:38.880 --> 00:59:40.160
It's just I'm making them today.

00:59:40.160 --> 00:59:44.350
So like Monday and Tuesdays, I used to, Sunday,
Monday, Tuesdays, I used to stream.

00:59:44.350 --> 00:59:46.320
Right now I have those open.

00:59:46.320 --> 00:59:51.260
So right now my Mondays are like my content
creation, my weekly planning.

00:59:51.260 --> 00:59:54.190
Tuesdays, I do some light hacking.

00:59:54.190 --> 00:59:57.880
And if I have Pentest, I do a lot of my Pentest,
Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursdays.

00:59:57.880 --> 01:00:00.240
And then Fridays is more of a content again.

01:00:00.240 --> 01:00:04.630
And then Saturdays are like my, I'm going
to catch up on whatever I didn't get to do.

01:00:04.630 --> 01:00:08.370
And then when I stream on Sundays, my only
rule, I don't work on Sundays other than my

01:00:08.370 --> 01:00:10.660
stream and my thumbnails for the next day.

01:00:10.660 --> 01:00:13.630
But other than that, I don't work on Sundays
at all.

01:00:13.630 --> 01:00:17.980
It's just the one day that I give myself to
do whatever I need.

01:00:17.980 --> 01:00:18.980
Okay.

01:00:18.980 --> 01:00:24.069
And what are some other activities that help
you just relax and chill out?

01:00:24.069 --> 01:00:27.160
I play a lot of Call of Duty Warzone.

01:00:27.160 --> 01:00:30.589
I don't know if that gives me the ability
to chill out because it stresses me out when

01:00:30.589 --> 01:00:32.500
I play the game.

01:00:32.500 --> 01:00:34.270
My buddy's like doing a dopamine detox.

01:00:34.270 --> 01:00:36.530
Like I'm not playing Call of Duty because
it gives me dopamine.

01:00:36.530 --> 01:00:37.750
I'm like, it gives you dopamine?

01:00:37.750 --> 01:00:41.160
It makes me stress, it gives me anxiety.

01:00:41.160 --> 01:00:43.790
But video games in general, it's a good one.

01:00:43.790 --> 01:00:45.740
I watch a lot of like TV shows.

01:00:45.740 --> 01:00:47.539
Sometimes I go on these binge watches.

01:00:47.539 --> 01:00:50.690
I'm going in the process of like re-watching
some of my favorite TV shows.

01:00:50.690 --> 01:00:51.690
And I'm like, oh, I'm going to binge watch
this.

01:00:51.690 --> 01:00:52.690
I'm going to binge watch this.

01:00:52.690 --> 01:00:53.690
I'm going to binge watch one of my favorite
TV shows.

01:00:53.690 --> 01:00:55.700
So I'm like, right now I'm watching Limitless.

01:00:55.700 --> 01:00:57.569
I just finished another show.

01:00:57.569 --> 01:01:00.400
I don't know what it was, but I'm just re-watching
some of my favorite shows.

01:01:00.400 --> 01:01:02.319
I'm going to re-watch Suits soon again.

01:01:02.319 --> 01:01:04.420
I love that show.

01:01:04.420 --> 01:01:07.579
Yeah, so just watch those, and then traveling
has been a good one.

01:01:07.579 --> 01:01:09.260
I tell people to spend their money.

01:01:09.260 --> 01:01:13.660
But for that reason, like go and travel and
go and do some things to remind yourself why

01:01:13.660 --> 01:01:14.950
you work so hard.

01:01:14.950 --> 01:01:15.950
Yeah.

01:01:15.950 --> 01:01:16.950
Yeah.

01:01:16.950 --> 01:01:17.950
That's a big one.

01:01:17.950 --> 01:01:21.460
What are your favorite travel destinations?

01:01:21.460 --> 01:01:23.380
I think, so I want to go to like Eastern Asia.

01:01:23.380 --> 01:01:28.350
I've never, I've only been to Singapore and
on the Asian side of the world.

01:01:28.350 --> 01:01:33.089
I really want to go to Japan and I want to
go to Thailand that I haven't been.

01:01:33.089 --> 01:01:39.400
But out of all the places I have been so far,
I'm always going to love Switzerland and some

01:01:39.400 --> 01:01:42.039
areas of France, some areas of France.

01:01:42.039 --> 01:01:46.220
I'm going to make that a very clear thing.

01:01:46.220 --> 01:01:47.580
But Europe has been a fun one.

01:01:47.580 --> 01:01:48.830
I've seen a lot of European countries.

01:01:48.830 --> 01:01:52.190
I'm excited to go to Hungary soon.

01:01:52.190 --> 01:01:55.369
I've seen, I want to go to Romania at some
point if I can.

01:01:55.369 --> 01:01:58.750
But favorite ones that I've ever seen, I want
to say Portugal was fun.

01:01:58.750 --> 01:02:01.950
I love going to Amsterdam once in a while.

01:02:01.950 --> 01:02:04.270
It's a really fun one.

01:02:04.270 --> 01:02:07.309
And Italy was also a little fun.

01:02:07.309 --> 01:02:08.500
I'm not going to lie.

01:02:08.500 --> 01:02:09.849
I had a blast in Italy.

01:02:09.849 --> 01:02:10.849
Yeah, there's a lot.

01:02:10.849 --> 01:02:13.260
There's a lot of countries you've been to.

01:02:13.260 --> 01:02:18.470
I've seen a lot of, this year alone, I think
I've done nine or 10 countries with work.

01:02:18.470 --> 01:02:19.470
Okay.

01:02:19.470 --> 01:02:20.470
Yeah.

01:02:20.470 --> 01:02:21.470
Yeah, that's great.

01:02:21.470 --> 01:02:27.750
Do you do like holidays without work or is
it more like workation type?

01:02:27.750 --> 01:02:32.130
So I'm taking my first ever holiday in August.

01:02:32.130 --> 01:02:35.700
Like I'm probably going on a holiday in like
years since COVID at least.

01:02:35.700 --> 01:02:39.579
Because COVID was like, there's no holidays,
right?

01:02:39.579 --> 01:02:41.840
Most of the time I have my laptop on me, even
when I went.

01:02:41.840 --> 01:02:42.930
So I'm Iranian.

01:02:42.930 --> 01:02:44.740
Iranian New Year's in March.

01:02:44.740 --> 01:02:48.760
When I went out there to see my family, I
worked out of the two weeks I was there, I

01:02:48.760 --> 01:02:51.000
worked like eight of them, nine of them at
least.

01:02:51.000 --> 01:02:52.740
And it wasn't like eight or nine days back
to back.

01:02:52.740 --> 01:02:54.950
It was like four days here, two days there,
one day there.

01:02:54.950 --> 01:02:58.490
You know, it was like, there was no span of
like having time off.

01:02:58.490 --> 01:03:04.020
So in August, I've told myself August 20th,
19th for a week, I don't care.

01:03:04.020 --> 01:03:06.250
Everything, unless it's going to burn, I'm
not going to touch it.

01:03:06.250 --> 01:03:09.890
So I'm going to have content ready to come
out, but I'm not going to be working at all.

01:03:09.890 --> 01:03:14.670
It's going to be a purification after all
the mad and craziness of DEF CON.

01:03:14.670 --> 01:03:16.099
Yeah, yeah.

01:03:16.099 --> 01:03:21.500
Congratulations on making this decision and
definitely will help you out later.

01:03:21.500 --> 01:03:23.510
I hope so, man.

01:03:23.510 --> 01:03:25.020
I hope so.

01:03:25.020 --> 01:03:29.090
When you create content, do you outsource
a lot of your work?

01:03:29.090 --> 01:03:34.810
